><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250604141941+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250604142012+10'00'" name="f785bc7b-23f9-412c-bd7b-c9b5210737f6" page="1" coords="418.023956,710.840210,544.254761,710.840210,418.023956,695.750305,544.254761,695.750305,306.143646,698.340210,544.051392,698.340210,306.143646,683.250305,544.051392,683.250305,306.143646,685.840210,544.254883,685.840210,306.143646,670.750305,544.254883,670.750305,306.143646,673.340210,385.828796,673.340210,306.143646,658.250305,385.828796,658.250305" rect="305.672089,657.778748,544.726440,711.311768" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q3: yes there was previous research on this topic. This article justified as more evidence is needed to change practice.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="1" rect="595.276001,618.840210,779.276001,710.840210"
/></highlight
><highlight color="#9643FC" opacity="0.399994" creationdate="D:20250604141358+10'00'" flags="print" IT="HighlightNote" date="D:20250604141519+10'00'" name="f14bd1dd-e825-42a5-b77c-61c576c8586e" page="6" coords="319.322815,136.813248,544.280396,136.813248,319.322815,121.723373,544.280396,121.723373,319.322815,124.313248,544.103760,124.313248,319.322815,109.223373,544.103760,109.223373,319.322815,111.813248,370.753479,111.813248,319.322815,96.723373,370.753479,96.723373,306.141693,99.299454,311.057861,99.299454,306.141693,85.114975,311.057861,85.114975,319.322815,99.313248,544.252808,99.313248,319.322815,84.223373,544.252808,84.223373,319.322845,86.813248,544.230408,86.813248,319.322845,71.723373,544.230408,71.723373" rect="305.698425,71.251816,544.751953,137.284805" subject="Comment on Text" title="alexd"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Topically applied menthol can improve TTE performance&#xD;for both single (high concentration) and repeated topical&#xD;applications.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Orally applied menthol improves TTE and TT performance&#xD;when used repeatedly throughout the exercise bout; there is less supporting evidence regarding singular&#xD;topical application.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>There is not sufficient evidence currently to support menthol’s&#xD;topical application in team-based sports; this is a promising avenue for future research.&#xD;&#xD;Menthol research has largely been conducted on male&#xD;cohorts who are recreationally active or trained.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Research on female participants is to be encouraged due&#xD;to potential variations in regional adiposity and sweat&#xD;rates amongst other factors.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>The use of menthol by elite athletes is currently not well&#xD;supported; however, its use is unlikely to be detrimental&#xD;(especially when mouth rinsing unless published protocols&#xD;regarding frequency and concentration are exceeded)&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Individual effectiveness in representative conditions for&#xD;forthcoming events should be trialled in the build-up to&#xD;major competitions to identify athlete specific benefits.&#xD;&#xD;&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Single and repeated menthol topical applications and&#xD;mouth rinsing reliably improve thermal sensation and, to&#xD;a lesser extent, thermal comfort and RPE during exercise&#xD;in a hot environment.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Menthol topical application may benefit activities where&#xD;heat tolerance is limiting, but may increase the risk of&#xD;heat-related illness.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Thermoeffector change with menthol topical application&#xD;is surface area, menthol concentration, and site-dependent,&#xD;with the chest, face, and neck suggested as the most&#xD;thermally sensitive areas. No such direct changes to body&#xD;temperature regulation are evident with mouth rinsing.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Even at low concentrations, menthol topical application&#xD;evokes changes in thermoeffector responses, which may&#xD;increase the risk of heat storage and have deleterious&#xD;effects on prolonged exercise in the heat.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Athletes should be familiar with testing protocols and&#xD;these should be well practiced prior to competitive use.&#xD;Awareness and education of the potential effects of menthol&#xD;are critical.&#xD;&#xD;&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Menthol is widely accessible and commercially available&#xD;in various forms, and thus, it does not permit unfair&#xD;sporting advantage to some potential users.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Menthol topical application and mouth rinsing probably&#xD;do not confer an unfair advantage.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Menthol topical application and mouth rinsing do not&#xD;violate the spirit of the sport.&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>• </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>When using menthol, athletes should avoid combining&#xD;menthol with other nutritional or pharmacological substances,&#xD;which may render it ineffective, harmful, or give&#xD;the potential for menthol to be contaminated with unapproved&#xD;substances.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="6" rect="595.276001,44.813248,779.276001,136.813248"
/></highlight
